\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} How far a selected set sits from a random set of the same size, in held-out Cohen's $d$. Every rule that keeps the allocation's \emph  {concentration} reproduces the separation --- permuting the uncertainty scores across clusters, and ignoring them entirely --- and only destroying concentration collapses it. Bars are means over four cells, whiskers the range. \textbf  {(b)} Trained $S_\alpha $ for the three arms that hold concentration \emph  {exactly} fixed and vary only the signal's direction; points are seeds, rules are arm means, the band is the $\pm 2\hat  {\sigma }$ bar. \emph  {Source:} \texttt  {c1\_attribution.csv} (\texttt  {EXP C1} \#4); \texttt  {out/t2/abc\_metrics.csv} (\texttt  {EXP T2} \#3).}}{8}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
